#!/usr/bin/env bash
#MISE description="Gate: the MCP startup budget is declared and is a measured budget, not the host default (CLOUD-668)"
#
# CLOUD-266's rule — "timeouts are uniform boilerplate, not measured budgets, so
# they bound nothing" — applied to the one budget nobody had looked at.
#
# Nothing in this repo set the MCP server startup budget, so it was the host's
# default 30000 ms. Serena is the only server it governs; the rest are HTTP
# connectors the host manages itself.
#
# WHAT EXCEEDING IT COSTS, which is why the margin has to be generous rather than
# tight. It is not a retry. Serena is absent for the WHOLE session, and the
# failure is legible only as "the known Serena flakiness":
#
#   * `mcp-attach-check` reports `serena CONNECT_TIMEOUT` truthfully, and a
#     session reads that and moves on. Measured: five times in one session.
#   * Serena owns `.serena/memories/**` through the protected-path gate, so an
#     absent Serena makes the memory tree unwritable for the rest of the session.
#     CLOUD-663 was opened to build a second write surface for exactly that, and
#     is Canceled as a workaround for this.
#   * The symbol tools go with it and navigation silently degrades to grep.
#
# THE MEASUREMENT, 2026-08-18. Full handshake as the client performs it —
# initialize, initialized, tools/list, prompts/list, resources/list:
#
#   warm, idle                              4.01s
#   under `mise run ci`, load avg 2.0-2.8   3.21s / 3.83s / 4.47s
#   host log, warm reconnect                5.39s
#   host log, COLD SESSION START           16.65s   <- worst observed SUCCESS
#   host log, re-registration x2          >30s, CONNECT_TIMEOUT
#
# `initialize` is the entire cost; the three list calls are 0.00-0.03s each.
#
# RE-MEASURED 2026-08-19 (CLOUD-700), and the worst observed SUCCESS moved by
# 3.2x. Two containers on the same commit, one per budget:
#
#   07:06 container, budget 30000    failed at 28162 ms, CONNECT_TIMEOUT
#   14:28 container, budget 120000   SUCCEEDED at 52747 ms
#
# The second number is the one that matters and it is why the floor moves below.
# 52747 ms is not an outlier to be explained away: it is a cold container paying
# a first rust-analyzer index (CLOUD-670) while provisioning runs, which is the
# ordinary shape of a session start here. Against the old floor of 60000 it had
# 1.14x headroom — tighter than the 1.8x this header already calls "not a budget,
# a coin flip". Against the declared 120000 it had 2.3x and attached.
#
# It also settles what the 07:06 failure was: not a broken Serena (it serves 21
# tools in 1.7 s by hand) and not contention (1.303 s under `mise run doctor`
# against 1.110 s idle), but a cold launch measured against the host default in a
# container where the declaration had not reached the client.
#
# WHY 120000 AND NOT 30000. Against the worst observed SUCCESS of 52747 ms the
# host default is not merely tight, it is BELOW the measurement: a connect that
# did succeed would have been killed by it, which is precisely the 07:06 row
# above. The cost function is deeply asymmetric — an over-long budget is paid
# only when the server is genuinely failing, once, at startup, whereas an
# under-set one costs every tool Serena owns for the rest of the session. 120000
# is 2.3x the worst observed success, which is the margin a cold container needs
# while it is also running `mise install`, a cargo build and a first-time
# rust-analyzer index.
#
# THE 16.65s THIS SECTION USED TO CITE was the 2026-08-18 worst success, and the
# "1.8x headroom" and "~7x" margins derived from it are superseded by the
# re-measurement above (CLOUD-730). They were not merely stale: 1.8x described
# the host default as tight when the later measurement shows it insufficient.
#
# The FLOOR sits below the declared value so it can be tuned down on evidence
# without editing this gate, while still refusing a return to the host default.
# Lowering the floor itself requires a new measurement in this header.
#
# It was 60000, derived from a worst observed success of 16650 ms. The 2026-08-19
# re-measurement above puts that at 52747 ms, which left the old floor at 1.14x —
# so it moves to 105494, exactly twice the worst observed success, keeping the
# declared 120000 above it and the tuning slack intact.
#
# NOT `.mcp.json`'s per-server `env`: that is passed to the CHILD, and this
# budget governs the PARENT's wait.
#
# BUT `.claude/settings.json`'s `env` BLOCK IS THE SURFACE, and it does reach the
# client — just not on every container, which is the whole reason this gate reads
# the log instead of the file.
#
# CORRECTED 2026-08-19 (CLOUD-730). This header previously concluded, from ten
# logs that all opened at `30000ms`, that the block "does not reach the MCP
# client", that BOTH in-repo surfaces were eliminated, and therefore that only
# the environment the CLI is launched with could set the budget — i.e. that no
# fix could live in this repository. That conclusion is FALSE, and the sample is
# why: every one of those ten logs predated the connection that settles it. The
# 14:28 container opened with `Starting connection with timeout of 120000ms`.
#
# WHAT IS STILL OPEN is why one container picks the declaration up and another
# does not. That question is live and this file does not answer it. Nothing here
# should be read as retiring the settings surface.
#
# THAT IS WHY THIS GATE ALSO ASSERTS THE EFFECT. Checking the declaration alone
# measures what the repo says while the failure is entirely in what the host
# does, and the cost of that gap was ten sessions of silence over a number
# somebody had already measured — the shape non-negotiable rule 2 exists to
# close. The observed budget is read from the MCP client's own connection log,
# which is the only artifact that states what actually applied.
#
# Pointer-only per non-negotiable rule 4: the declared value and the floor.
#
# Exit 0 declared and at or above the floor / 1 absent or below it / 2 could not
# look.
#
# The mutation accepts any declared value, dropping the floor comparison. The
# host default 30000 is a declared value, so a gate that only checks presence
# passes the exact state CLOUD-668 was filed about.
#MUTANT presence-is-not-a-budget|s/\[ "\$declared" -ge "\$FLOOR" \]/[ -n "$declared" ]/|a value below the floor is refused, and both numbers are named
# The second mutation drops the effect half, leaving the declaration check alone
# — which passes on any container where the client used the host default while
# this file declared 120000, the exact 07:06 case above. Only a row that reads
# the LOG can catch it.
#MUTANT declaration-without-effect|s/\[ "\$observed" -lt "\$declared" \]/false/|an observed budget below the declared one is refused
# The third mutation drops the floor's own arithmetic, so a floor may be moved
# without moving the measurement that justifies it — the drift that let a
# superseded "16.65s" go on justifying a number derived from 52747 ms. Only a row
# that declares a basis disagreeing with its floor can catch it.
#MUTANT floor-without-basis|s/\[ "\$FLOOR" -ne \$((budget_worst \* budget_multiplier)) \]/false/|a floor disagreeing with its declared basis is refused
#PIN-OK: jq
set -uo pipefail

# THE FLOOR AND THE MEASUREMENT THAT JUSTIFIES IT, on one line, in the form
# `timeout-check` already uses for workflow jobs (CLOUD-266):
#
#   FLOOR=<ms> # budget: worst=<ms>ms x<multiplier> measured=YYYY-MM-DD
#
# and it is PARSED below rather than merely readable. A floor moved without its
# basis fails, and so does a basis moved without the floor — which is exactly how
# a "16.65s" justification outlived the number it justified (CLOUD-730). Raising
# the declared value in settings is free; moving this needs a new row in the
# measurement table above and the arithmetic to match it.
#
# 105494 rather than the 105000 that first landed: 52747 x 2 is 105494, and the
# rounding that produced the older value is a small instance of the same drift
# this assertion exists to refuse.
FLOOR=105494 # budget: worst=52747ms x2 measured=2026-08-19

# Parsed from the file the assignment above lives in, so a fixture can exercise
# every direction of the check by declaring its own line. `timeout-check` is a
# pure function of the workflows it reads; this is a pure function of one line of
# this file.
budget_file="${MCP_TIMEOUT_BUDGET:-${BASH_SOURCE[0]}}"
if [ ! -r "$budget_file" ]; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: cannot read $budget_file to check the floor's own budget comment" >&2
	exit 2
fi
budget_line=$(grep -m1 -E '^FLOOR=' "$budget_file")
budget_re='^FLOOR=([0-9]+)[[:space:]]#[[:space:]]budget:[[:space:]]worst=([0-9]+)ms[[:space:]]x([0-9]+)[[:space:]]measured=[0-9]{4}-[0-9]{2}-[0-9]{2}$'
if [[ ! $budget_line =~ $budget_re ]]; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: the floor carries no parsable budget comment — a limit with no recorded measurement is the boilerplate CLOUD-266 refuses" >&2
	echo "  want FLOOR=<ms> # budget: worst=<ms>ms x<n> measured=YYYY-MM-DD" >&2
	exit 1
fi
FLOOR="${BASH_REMATCH[1]}"
budget_worst="${BASH_REMATCH[2]}"
budget_multiplier="${BASH_REMATCH[3]}"
if [ "$FLOOR" -ne $((budget_worst * budget_multiplier)) ]; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: the floor disagrees with the basis it declares" >&2
	echo "  floor $FLOOR" >&2
	echo "  basis $budget_worst x$budget_multiplier = $((budget_worst * budget_multiplier))" >&2
	exit 1
fi

settings="${MCP_TIMEOUT_SETTINGS:-.claude/settings.json}"
logroot="${MCP_TIMEOUT_LOGS:-}"
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
	case "$1" in
	# `shift 2` on a single remaining argument SHIFTS NOTHING and returns
	# non-zero, and `errexit` is off in this file — so `--settings` with no value
	# left `$1` unchanged and spun the loop forever. A gate that hangs is worse
	# than one that fails: `ci-wait` and the hk gate both wait on it.
	--settings)
		if [ $# -lt 2 ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
			echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: --settings needs a file" >&2
			exit 2
		fi
		settings="$2"
		shift 2
		;;
	# Same argument shape as `--settings`, and the same reason for the explicit
	# arity check: `shift 2` on a lone flag shifts nothing and spins the loop.
	--logs)
		if [ $# -lt 2 ] || [ -z "$2" ]; then
			echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: --logs needs a directory" >&2
			exit 2
		fi
		logroot="$2"
		shift 2
		;;
	*)
		echo "usage: mcp-timeout-budget [--settings <file>] [--logs <dir>]" >&2
		exit 2
		;;
	esac
done

if ! command -v jq >/dev/null 2>&1; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: no jq on PATH — cannot read $settings, and a gate that cannot look must not report a budget it did not check" >&2
	exit 2
fi
if [ ! -r "$settings" ]; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: cannot read $settings" >&2
	exit 2
fi

declared=$(jq -r '.env.MCP_TIMEOUT // empty' "$settings" 2>/dev/null) || {
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: $settings is not readable JSON" >&2
	exit 2
}

if [ -z "$declared" ]; then
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: no env.MCP_TIMEOUT in $settings — the budget is the host default, which is not a measured budget (CLOUD-266, CLOUD-668)" >&2
	echo "  floor $FLOOR" >&2
	exit 1
fi

case "$declared" in
'' | *[!0-9]*)
	echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: env.MCP_TIMEOUT in $settings is not a whole number of milliseconds" >&2
	exit 1
	;;
esac

# --- the effect half (CLOUD-700) ---------------------------------------------
#
# The declaration is necessary and was never sufficient. This asks the only
# artifact that knows: the CLI writes one connection log per attempt, and its
# first record states the budget it actually applied.
#
#   {"debug":"Starting connection with timeout of 30000ms", …}
#
# FAIL OPEN on a missing log ROOT, and only there — no root means this is not a
# live CLI session (CI, a bare checkout, another harness), and a gate that cannot
# look must not report a verdict it did not compute. That is `mcp-attach-check`'s
# posture on the same tree, deliberately, so the two agree about when there is
# nothing to see.
#
# A server directory that EXISTS but records no budget is exit 2 rather than a
# pass: the tree is live, the question is answerable in principle, and something
# about this reading is not what either gate understands.
observed_budget_holds() {
	local root="$1" servers="$2" server dir newest observed
	[ -d "$root" ] || {
		echo "mcp-timeout-budget: no MCP log tree at $root — not a live session, the declared budget is all there is to check"
		return 0
	}
	while IFS= read -r server; do
		[ -n "$server" ] || continue
		dir="$root/mcp-logs-$server"
		[ -d "$dir" ] || continue
		# Newest by NAME: each attempt opens a file named for its UTC start
		# instant, a fixed-width ISO-8601 form whose lexicographic order is its
		# chronological one. `mcp-attach-check` picks this session's log the same
		# way, and for the reason stated there — mtime tracks last append, the
		# name records which attempt the file belongs to.
		newest=$(find "$dir" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.jsonl' 2>/dev/null | sort -r | head -n 1)
		[ -n "$newest" ] && [ -r "$newest" ] || continue
		observed=$(grep -oE 'Starting connection with timeout of [0-9]+ms' "$newest" 2>/dev/null |
			head -n 1 | grep -oE '[0-9]+')
		if [ -z "$observed" ]; then
			echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: $server's newest log records no startup budget, so what the client applied cannot be read" >&2
			return 2
		fi
		if [ "$observed" -lt "$declared" ]; then
			echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: the host ignored the declared MCP startup budget" >&2
			echo "  $server observed $observed" >&2
			echo "  $settings env.MCP_TIMEOUT $declared" >&2
			echo "  A declared budget the client does not use is not a budget. Why this container used a different one is OPEN (CLOUD-730): the declaration in this settings file demonstrably does reach the client on other containers, so do not 'fix' this by moving the key out of the repository. Serena had the observed budget, not the declared one, to attach in." >&2
			return 1
		fi
	done <<<"$servers"
	return 0
}

if [ "$declared" -ge "$FLOOR" ]; then
	if [ -z "$logroot" ]; then
		logroot="${HOME}/.cache/claude-cli-nodejs/$(pwd | tr '/' '-')"
	fi
	enabled=$(jq -r '(.enabledMcpjsonServers // empty) | if type == "array" then .[] else empty end' "$settings" 2>/dev/null) || enabled=""
	if [ -n "$enabled" ]; then
		observed_budget_holds "$logroot" "$enabled" || exit $?
	fi
	echo "mcp-timeout-budget: MCP_TIMEOUT $declared ms, at or above the measured floor $FLOOR ms, and in force where a log records it"
	exit 0
fi

echo "::error:: mcp-timeout-budget: the MCP startup budget is below its measured floor" >&2
echo "  $settings env.MCP_TIMEOUT $declared" >&2
echo "  floor $FLOOR" >&2
echo "  Serena's worst observed successful connect is 52747 ms on a cold container (2026-08-19). A budget that close to it loses the whole session's Serena when it is missed — see this task's header for the measurement table." >&2
exit 1
